1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,970
The second of September 1945,

2
00:00:02,990 --> 00:00:05,816
aboard the battleship Missouri in Tokyo Bay.

3
00:00:05,836 --> 00:00:07,848
The Japanese delegation has signed.

4
00:00:07,868 --> 00:00:10,288
General Douglas MacArthur steps to the

5
00:00:10,308 --> 00:00:12,320
table and calls two names.

6
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:14,353
Will General Percival and General

7
00:00:14,373 --> 00:00:16,792
Wainwright step forward and accompany me

8
00:00:16,812 --> 00:00:18,012
while I sign?

9
00:00:18,032 --> 00:00:21,264
A thin officer with white hair walks out

10
00:00:21,284 --> 00:00:24,130
of the crowd and stands behind him.

11
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:27,812
MacArthur sits down and begins to write

12
00:00:27,832 --> 00:00:28,521
his name.

13
00:00:28,541 --> 00:00:29,940
Halfway through, he stops,

14
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:32,776
turns around, and hands the pen to the

15
00:00:32,796 --> 00:00:34,195
thin man behind him.

16
00:00:34,215 --> 00:00:36,322
Three years earlier, MacArthur had written

17
00:00:36,342 --> 00:00:38,805
to Washington that giving this same man

18
00:00:38,825 --> 00:00:41,996
the Medal of Honor would be a grave injustice.

19
00:00:42,016 --> 00:00:44,833
And for three years, in prison camps from

20
00:00:44,853 --> 00:00:46,251
the Philippines to Manchuria,

21
00:00:46,271 --> 00:00:48,733
this man had believed that America blamed

22
00:00:48,753 --> 00:00:51,590
him for the worst surrender in its history.

23
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:55,565
This is the documented story of General

24
00:00:55,585 --> 00:00:56,346
Jonathan Wainwright:

25
00:00:56,366 --> 00:00:58,690
the man MacArthur left behind on

26
00:00:58,710 --> 00:01:01,195
Corregidor, the surrender he never forgave

27
00:01:01,215 --> 00:01:03,616
himself for, and the day he found out what

28
00:01:03,636 --> 00:01:05,250
his country really thought of him.

29
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:09,900
March 1942.

30
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:11,800
The Philippines are cut off.

31
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:14,740
On the island fortress of Corregidor,

32
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:17,040
at the mouth of Manila Bay,

33
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:18,957
MacArthur has orders from President

34
00:01:18,977 --> 00:01:20,873
Roosevelt to leave for Australia.

35
00:01:20,893 --> 00:01:23,557
Before he goes, he calls in Wainwright,

36
00:01:23,577 --> 00:01:26,260
the lanky old cavalryman everyone calls Skinny.

37
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:30,071
MacArthur tells him to say to the troops

38
00:01:30,091 --> 00:01:32,382
that he is leaving on the president's

39
00:01:32,402 --> 00:01:34,363
repeated orders, and against his own

40
00:01:34,383 --> 00:01:35,023
repeated protests.

41
00:01:35,043 --> 00:01:37,994
He promises to come back as soon as he

42
00:01:38,014 --> 00:01:40,305
can, with as much as he can.

43
00:01:40,325 --> 00:01:42,946
He hands Wainwright a box of cigars and

44
00:01:42,966 --> 00:01:44,597
two jars of shaving cream.

45
00:01:44,617 --> 00:01:46,907
And he promises him a third star,

46
00:01:46,927 --> 00:01:49,879
if he is still on Bataan when MacArthur returns.

47
00:01:49,899 --> 00:01:52,189
Wainwright answers that he will be there,

48
00:01:52,209 --> 00:01:53,530
if he is alive.

49
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:57,005
That night, MacArthur's PT boats slip

50
00:01:57,025 --> 00:01:59,179
through the minefields in the dark.

51
00:01:59,199 --> 00:02:00,991
A week later, in Australia,

52
00:02:01,011 --> 00:02:02,079
he tells reporters:

53
00:02:02,099 --> 00:02:04,616
I came through, and I shall return.

54
00:02:04,636 --> 00:02:07,153
Behind him, Wainwright is left in command

55
00:02:07,173 --> 00:02:09,690
of a starving army on Bataan and

56
00:02:09,710 --> 00:02:11,990
Corregidor, with no help on the way.

57
00:02:13,675 --> 00:02:15,875
On the ninth of April, Bataan falls.

58
00:02:15,895 --> 00:02:19,801
Corregidor holds on for four more weeks

59
00:02:19,821 --> 00:02:20,875
under Japanese artillery.

60
00:02:20,895 --> 00:02:23,736
On the morning of the sixth of May,

61
00:02:23,756 --> 00:02:26,240
Wainwright makes the decision of his life.

62
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:28,745
He will sacrifice, as the Army's official

63
00:02:28,765 --> 00:02:31,606
history puts it, one day of freedom in

64
00:02:31,626 --> 00:02:33,415
exchange for several thousand lives.

65
00:02:34,725 --> 00:02:37,141
He sends a last message to Roosevelt.

66
00:02:37,161 --> 00:02:39,577
With broken heart and head bowed in

67
00:02:39,597 --> 00:02:42,013
sadness but not in shame, he writes,

68
00:02:42,033 --> 00:02:44,797
my troops and I have accomplished all that

69
00:02:44,817 --> 00:02:45,841
is humanly possible.

70
00:02:45,861 --> 00:02:49,975
At noon, the white flag goes up over Corregidor.

71
00:02:50,675 --> 00:02:53,957
But that is not enough for the Japanese.

72
00:02:53,977 --> 00:02:56,855
That afternoon, at Cabcaben on Bataan,

73
00:02:56,875 --> 00:02:59,525
General Masaharu Homma refuses to accept

74
00:02:59,545 --> 00:03:01,943
the surrender of Corregidor alone.

75
00:03:01,963 --> 00:03:04,633
He wants every American and Filipino

76
00:03:04,653 --> 00:03:06,426
soldier in the Philippines.

77
00:03:06,446 --> 00:03:09,115
When Wainwright tries to limit it,

78
00:03:09,135 --> 00:03:11,822
Homma tells him he will only negotiate

79
00:03:11,842 --> 00:03:12,955
with his equal.

80
00:03:12,975 --> 00:03:15,222
Wainwright thinks of the eleven thousand

81
00:03:15,242 --> 00:03:17,175
disarmed men on Corregidor,

82
00:03:17,195 --> 00:03:18,159
and gives in.

83
00:03:18,179 --> 00:03:20,781
The next night, in a Manila radio studio,

84
00:03:20,801 --> 00:03:23,404
he reads the order to surrender to the

85
00:03:23,424 --> 00:03:25,699
whole country, in a voice husky with

86
00:03:25,719 --> 00:03:26,519
suppressed emotion.

87
00:03:28,225 --> 00:03:30,799
In Australia, MacArthur's reaction is cold.

88
00:03:30,819 --> 00:03:32,529
Orders from General Wainwright,

89
00:03:32,549 --> 00:03:35,988
he radios to his commanders in the south,

90
00:03:36,008 --> 00:03:37,285
have no validity.

91
00:03:37,305 --> 00:03:40,312
To General Marshall, the Army's chief of

92
00:03:40,332 --> 00:03:42,041
staff, he goes further.

93
00:03:42,061 --> 00:03:45,068
I believe, he writes, that Wainwright has

94
00:03:45,088 --> 00:03:46,385
temporarily become unbalanced.

95
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:49,681
Two months later, Marshall proposes

96
00:03:49,701 --> 00:03:52,083
Wainwright for the Medal of Honor.

97
00:03:52,103 --> 00:03:54,885
MacArthur answers with a radiogram that is

98
00:03:54,905 --> 00:03:56,486
still in the files.

99
00:03:56,506 --> 00:03:58,487
The citation proposed, he writes,

100
00:03:58,507 --> 00:04:00,489
does not represent the truth.

101
00:04:00,509 --> 00:04:03,691
Giving it to Wainwright would be a grave

102
00:04:03,711 --> 00:04:06,493
injustice to other generals who had shown

103
00:04:06,513 --> 00:04:08,494
leadership greatly superior to his.

104
00:04:08,514 --> 00:04:10,896
It would be a grave mistake,

105
00:04:10,916 --> 00:04:13,698
MacArthur warns, which later on might well

106
00:04:13,718 --> 00:04:15,299
lead to embarrassing repercussions.

107
00:04:15,319 --> 00:04:16,920
Marshall withdraws the recommendation.

108
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:20,836
Wainwright never sees any of this.

109
00:04:20,856 --> 00:04:22,820
He is already a prisoner.

110
00:04:24,575 --> 00:04:26,155
He is held first at Tarlac,

111
00:04:26,175 --> 00:04:29,328
on Luzon, with the other captured generals.

112
00:04:29,348 --> 00:04:32,715
In August 1942 the Japanese ship them to

113
00:04:32,735 --> 00:04:35,147
Karenko, on Formosa, By October,

114
00:04:35,167 --> 00:04:37,027
Wainwright weighs one hundred and

115
00:04:37,047 --> 00:04:39,283
thirty-two pounds, forty pounds under his

116
00:04:39,303 --> 00:04:40,103
normal weight.

117
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:44,245
At Karenko, a Japanese correspondent asks

118
00:04:44,265 --> 00:04:47,335
him a question he cannot stop thinking about.

119
00:04:47,355 --> 00:04:48,880
Will you be court-martialed,

120
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:50,425
he asks, for surrendering?

121
00:04:50,445 --> 00:04:53,129
Wainwright answers that he acted on the

122
00:04:53,149 --> 00:04:55,447
authority of his commander in chief.

123
00:04:55,467 --> 00:04:58,537
But afterwards he sits down with his diary

124
00:04:58,557 --> 00:05:00,468
and writes out his defense,

125
00:05:00,488 --> 00:05:01,627
reason by reason.

126
00:05:01,647 --> 00:05:03,172
No help was coming.

127
00:05:03,192 --> 00:05:06,262
The island would have fallen within a day

128
00:05:06,282 --> 00:05:07,035
or two.

129
00:05:07,055 --> 00:05:09,957
In the tunnels of Corregidor lay a

130
00:05:09,977 --> 00:05:11,451
thousand sick and wounded men,

131
00:05:11,471 --> 00:05:13,243
and more than a hundred nurses.

132
00:05:13,263 --> 00:05:15,035
If he had sacrificed them all,

133
00:05:15,055 --> 00:05:17,425
he wrote, some would have called him a

134
00:05:17,445 --> 00:05:18,640
fool, or a criminal.

135
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:21,828
He is moved again, and again:

136
00:05:21,848 --> 00:05:23,940
to other camps on Formosa,

137
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:26,849
then through Japan and Korea to Manchuria.

138
00:05:26,869 --> 00:05:30,429
In December 1944, he and fifteen other

139
00:05:30,449 --> 00:05:32,740
senior prisoners end up at Sian,

140
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:34,712
a small camp in the cold north,

141
00:05:34,732 --> 00:05:37,250
about a hundred miles beyond Mukden.

142
00:05:37,950 --> 00:05:40,513
If you want more prisoner of war stories

143
00:05:40,533 --> 00:05:43,230
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

144
00:05:43,250 --> 00:05:43,699
Frontlines.

145
00:05:43,719 --> 00:05:46,530
It helps us keep finding them.

146
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:50,910
On the sixteenth of August 1945,

147
00:05:50,930 --> 00:05:53,288
the day after Japan announced its

148
00:05:53,308 --> 00:05:56,063
surrender, a six-man team from the OSS,

149
00:05:56,083 --> 00:05:57,648
America's wartime intelligence service,

150
00:05:57,668 --> 00:06:01,030
parachutes onto the airfield at Mukden.

151
00:06:01,050 --> 00:06:03,179
Their mission is to find the prisoners.

152
00:06:03,199 --> 00:06:05,636
Three days later, two of them reach the

153
00:06:05,656 --> 00:06:07,150
camp at Sian:

154
00:06:07,170 --> 00:06:08,800
an Army doctor, Major Lamar,

155
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:11,690
and an interpreter named Hal Leith.

156
00:06:11,710 --> 00:06:13,361
They are the first free Americans

157
00:06:13,381 --> 00:06:17,250
Wainwright has seen since the sixth of May 1942.

158
00:06:17,950 --> 00:06:19,901
He has one question for them.

159
00:06:19,921 --> 00:06:22,530
What do people at home think of him?

160
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:25,243
Leith later remembered that Wainwright

161
00:06:25,263 --> 00:06:27,997
expected to be blamed for the surrender.

162
00:06:28,017 --> 00:06:31,143
The Americans tell him he is a hero.

163
00:06:31,163 --> 00:06:33,130
He does not believe them.

164
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:37,464
It takes them a week to get out.

165
00:06:37,484 --> 00:06:40,206
A Soviet column, rain, a derailed train.

166
00:06:40,226 --> 00:06:43,370
They reach Mukden at one in the morning on

167
00:06:43,390 --> 00:06:45,957
the twenty-seventh and fly out the same day.

168
00:06:45,977 --> 00:06:47,573
On the twenty-eighth of August,

169
00:06:47,593 --> 00:06:49,513
in western China, Wainwright sees the

170
00:06:49,533 --> 00:06:52,423
American flag go up for the first time in

171
00:06:52,443 --> 00:06:53,243
thirty-nine months.

172
00:06:54,825 --> 00:06:57,129
Two days later, MacArthur lands in Japan

173
00:06:57,149 --> 00:06:59,785
in his plane, which he had named Bataan.

174
00:06:59,805 --> 00:07:02,109
On the evening of the thirty-first of

175
00:07:02,129 --> 00:07:04,433
August, at the New Grand Hotel in

176
00:07:04,453 --> 00:07:06,425
Yokohama, Wainwright walks into the dining

177
00:07:06,445 --> 00:07:08,749
room, leaning on the cane MacArthur had

178
00:07:08,769 --> 00:07:11,325
left him in 1942.

179
00:07:12,025 --> 00:07:14,669
What happened next depends on who tells it.

180
00:07:14,689 --> 00:07:17,000
In his own account, published that fall,

181
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:19,664
Wainwright says both men found it hard to

182
00:07:19,684 --> 00:07:22,329
speak, and that MacArthur only said he was

183
00:07:22,349 --> 00:07:23,661
glad to see him.

184
00:07:23,681 --> 00:07:25,992
In his memoirs, nearly twenty years later,

185
00:07:26,012 --> 00:07:27,657
MacArthur wrote that Wainwright was

186
00:07:27,677 --> 00:07:29,655
haggard and aged, his hair white,

187
00:07:29,675 --> 00:07:31,653
his skin like old shoe leather,

188
00:07:31,673 --> 00:07:33,318
and that his voice failed.

189
00:07:33,338 --> 00:07:35,316
He thought he was in disgrace,

190
00:07:35,336 --> 00:07:37,980
MacArthur wrote, and he told him his old

191
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,312
corps was his whenever he wanted it.

192
00:07:40,332 --> 00:07:42,643
A general sitting at the table remembered

193
00:07:42,663 --> 00:07:44,974
that MacArthur rose at once and embraced

194
00:07:44,994 --> 00:07:47,325
him, and that Wainwright broke into tears.

195
00:07:48,675 --> 00:07:51,307
The photograph taken that night survives.

196
00:07:51,327 --> 00:07:53,518
MacArthur is gripping Wainwright's arms.

197
00:07:53,538 --> 00:07:54,844
Wainwright is smiling.

198
00:07:54,864 --> 00:07:59,265
It is the face of a man who had spent

199
00:07:59,285 --> 00:08:01,495
three years expecting the opposite.

200
00:08:03,225 --> 00:08:05,613
Two days later, he stands behind MacArthur

201
00:08:05,633 --> 00:08:07,676
on the deck of the Missouri.

202
00:08:07,696 --> 00:08:09,396
According to Wainwright's own account,

203
00:08:09,416 --> 00:08:12,148
MacArthur took five pens out of his pocket.

204
00:08:12,168 --> 00:08:14,211
Halfway through his signature he stopped

205
00:08:14,231 --> 00:08:16,619
and handed the first pen to Wainwright.

206
00:08:16,639 --> 00:08:19,715
Then he wrote a little more and gave the

207
00:08:19,735 --> 00:08:21,434
next one to General Percival,

208
00:08:21,454 --> 00:08:23,498
the British commander who had surrendered

209
00:08:23,518 --> 00:08:23,842
Singapore.

210
00:08:23,862 --> 00:08:26,938
Two men who had signed two of the biggest

211
00:08:26,958 --> 00:08:29,689
surrenders of the war now stood at the

212
00:08:29,709 --> 00:08:31,085
front of the victory.

213
00:08:32,375 --> 00:08:35,459
The next day Wainwright flew back to the

214
00:08:35,479 --> 00:08:35,847
Philippines.

215
00:08:35,867 --> 00:08:38,500
At Baguio, in the mountains of Luzon,

216
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:40,570
the Japanese commander in the islands,

217
00:08:40,590 --> 00:08:43,185
General Yamashita, surrendered his forces.

218
00:08:43,205 --> 00:08:46,200
Wainwright sat at the center of the table.

219
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:48,461
The American general who took the

220
00:08:48,481 --> 00:08:51,495
surrender handed him one of his pens too.

221
00:08:53,175 --> 00:08:56,035
On the fifth of September 1945,

222
00:08:56,055 --> 00:08:58,044
Marshall put Wainwright's name forward for

223
00:08:58,064 --> 00:09:00,052
the Medal of Honor once again.

224
00:09:00,072 --> 00:09:02,061
This time, according to the Army's

225
00:09:02,081 --> 00:09:03,755
records, there were no objections.

226
00:09:05,225 --> 00:09:08,271
On the tenth, Wainwright landed in Washington.

227
00:09:08,291 --> 00:09:11,336
More than a hundred thousand people packed

228
00:09:11,356 --> 00:09:14,402
the grounds of the Washington Monument to

229
00:09:14,422 --> 00:09:15,277
hear him.

230
00:09:15,297 --> 00:09:17,467
Remember Bataan, he told them.

231
00:09:17,487 --> 00:09:19,885
Remember Corregidor.

232
00:09:19,905 --> 00:09:22,405
Then he went to the White House.

233
00:09:22,425 --> 00:09:24,925
President Truman suggested a walk in the

234
00:09:24,945 --> 00:09:26,365
garden, for the photographers.

235
00:09:26,385 --> 00:09:28,525
In the Rose Garden, to Wainwright's

236
00:09:28,545 --> 00:09:31,045
surprise, Truman read out a citation and

237
00:09:31,065 --> 00:09:33,925
fastened the Medal of Honor around his neck.

238
00:09:33,945 --> 00:09:36,805
Three days later, New York gave him a

239
00:09:36,825 --> 00:09:37,625
ticker-tape parade.

240
00:09:38,925 --> 00:09:40,567
In his private diary that June,

241
00:09:40,587 --> 00:09:42,505
Truman had written that America would have

242
00:09:42,525 --> 00:09:44,997
had a real general and a fighting man if

243
00:09:45,017 --> 00:09:46,125
it had had Wainwright.

244
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:50,140
If someone in your family was on Bataan or

245
00:09:50,160 --> 00:09:52,376
Corregidor, or in the camps with

246
00:09:52,396 --> 00:09:53,640
Wainwright, tell us in the comments.

247
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:57,297
Jonathan Wainwright commanded the Fourth

248
00:09:57,317 --> 00:10:02,100
Army after the war and retired in 1947.

249
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:04,050
When he told his story, he kept the

250
00:10:04,070 --> 00:10:06,020
cigars, the cane and the pen in it.

251
00:10:07,150 --> 00:10:10,550
He died on the second of September 1953,

252
00:10:10,570 --> 00:10:13,441
eight years to the day after he stood

253
00:10:13,461 --> 00:10:15,249
behind MacArthur on the Missouri.

254
00:10:15,269 --> 00:10:18,497
In 1942, MacArthur had warned that

255
00:10:18,517 --> 00:10:20,923
honoring him would be a grave mistake.

256
00:10:20,943 --> 00:10:24,450
In 1945, MacArthur handed him the first pen.

